  "biography": {
    "plain_text": "Courtney Sale Ross was an American filmmaker and education philanthropist who co-founded Ross School in East Hampton with her husband, Time Warner executive Steve Ross. Raised in Texas and educated at Skidmore, she worked in galleries and documentaries before developing the school. She inherited a substantial share of her husband's estate.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 94,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Primary institutional life account supports career and formation; contemporary estate reporting supports inheritance."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S2",
      "S3"
    ],
    "word_count": 51,
    "html": "<p>Courtney Sale Ross was an American filmmaker and education philanthropist who co-founded Ross School in East Hampton with her husband, Time Warner executive Steve Ross. Raised in Texas and educated at Skidmore, she worked in galleries and documentaries before developing the school. She inherited a substantial share of her husband's estate.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "An exhibition nearing its closing date prompted Courtney Sale Ross to make her first film. She had organised a survey of New York art at the state museum in Albany. With two weeks remaining, she decided to preserve it on film, creating The Big Picture. Further documentaries explored the lives and working methods of abstract expressionist artists.\n\nAt Ross School, she connected subjects through cultural history. Pupils studied successive civilisations, examining their art and literature alongside science, mathematics and technology. She designed the final-year curriculum around art history and individual projects, allowing students to investigate subjects that particularly interested them.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 94,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "College feature explains the precise filmmaking decision; institutional obituary describes curriculum mechanism and her personal final-year contribution."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S3"
    ],
    "word_count": 100,
    "html": "<p>An exhibition nearing its closing date prompted Courtney Sale Ross to make her first film. She had organised a survey of New York art at the state museum in Albany. With two weeks remaining, she decided to preserve it on film, creating The Big Picture. Further documentaries explored the lives and working methods of abstract expressionist artists.</p>\r\n<p>At Ross School, she connected subjects through cultural history. Pupils studied successive civilisations, examining their art and literature alongside science, mathematics and technology. She designed the final-year curriculum around art history and individual projects, allowing students to investigate subjects that particularly interested them.</p>\r\n"
  },
